---
import ArticleLayout from '../../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro';
import InShort from '../../../components/article/InShort.astro';
import ZenTip from '../../../components/article/ZenTip.astro';
import ZenPicture from '../../../components/article/ZenPicture.astro';

const card = 'rounded-2xl border border-[color:var(--color-border)] p-4';
const cardTitle = 'font-extrabold text-[color:var(--color-ink)]';
const cardText = 'mt-1 text-[0.9375rem] leading-snug text-[color:var(--color-ink)]';
const cardMuted = 'mt-1 text-[0.9375rem] leading-snug text-[color:var(--color-ink-muted)]';
---
<ArticleLayout title="How to Track Cortisol Levels at Home: Testing Guide" description="Compare home saliva and urine collection with blood testing. Learn why timing, instructions and clinical interpretation matter—and what apps cannot measure." updated="2026-11-12">
<InShort points={[
  'Some cortisol tests let you collect <strong>saliva or urine at home</strong>.',
  'Follow the <strong>exact instructions</strong> and timing you were given.',
  'An app or watch score <strong>can\'t measure cortisol</strong>.',
]} />

<p><strong>Some cortisol tests let you collect saliva or urine at home, following a doctor's or lab's instructions.</strong> The sample is then used to measure the hormone.</p>
<p>A phone questionnaire, a heart rate variability (HRV) reading or an Apple Watch wellness score can't measure your cortisol level.</p>
<p>Cortisol+ publishes this guide to help you learn. We don't sell a cortisol test, and our app does not replace lab testing.</p>

<h2>How to track cortisol levels at home</h2>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Agree on the question with a doctor.</strong> The right test depends on what's being looked into. Feeling stressed doesn't on its own point to a hormone disorder.</li>
  <li><strong>Confirm how and when to collect.</strong> Ask whether a saliva or urine sample collected at home is right for you, and how it will be tested.</li>
  <li><strong>Follow the exact instructions.</strong> Use the collection, storage and return steps you're given. Tell the doctor about your medicines and your sleep schedule.</li>
  <li><strong>Keep a collection record.</strong> Write down the date, time, sample type and anything you did differently from the instructions.</li>
  <li><strong>Look at the result in context.</strong> Use the lab's units and reference range, and ask whether you need a follow-up test.</li>
</ol>
<p><a href="https://medlineplus.gov/lab-tests/cortisol-test/">MedlinePlus's cortisol-test guide</a> explains blood, urine and saliva testing. Don't swap the plan you were given for a general four-sample schedule.</p>

<ZenTip image="/app-ui/zen_new_tour_now.webp">
  Set a <strong>phone alarm</strong> for each collection time. Then write down the time you actually collected.
</ZenTip>

<h2>How is home collection different from lab testing?</h2>
<p><strong>Each method works differently, and the timing and purpose matter.</strong></p>
<div class="not-prose my-6 grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-2">
  <div class={card}>
    <p class={cardTitle}>Saliva</p>
    <p class={cardText}>You may collect a sample at home at set times.</p>
    <p class={cardMuted}>Timing and purpose matter. No single kit is best for every question.</p>
  </div>
  <div class={card}>
    <p class={cardTitle}>24-hour urine</p>
    <p class={cardText}>You collect urine over the set period, and a lab tests it.</p>
    <p class={cardMuted}>Following the full plan matters. Ask what to do if you miss a collection.</p>
  </div>
  <div class={card}>
    <p class={cardTitle}>Blood</p>
    <p class={cardText}>A healthcare professional takes a blood sample, sometimes as part of a wider set of tests.</p>
    <p class={cardMuted}>A random result is not the same as a timed medical test.</p>
  </div>
  <div class={card}>
    <p class={cardTitle}>Wearable or journal</p>
    <p class={cardText}>Records body signals or your own notes.</p>
    <p class={cardMuted}>Doesn't measure cortisol. It is not a fourth kind of hormone test.</p>
  </div>
</div>
<p><a href="https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/endocrine-diseases/cushings-syndrome">NIDDK describes how doctors look into suspected Cushing's syndrome</a>, including urine, saliva and suppression tests.</p>
<p>The medical question decides the approach. This is not advice that everyone should test themselves routinely.</p>

<h2 id="collection-record">A collection record that makes follow-up easier</h2>
<p><a href="https://medlineplus.gov/lab-tests/cortisol-test/">MedlinePlus explains that how you prepare depends on the test</a>, and that saliva may be collected at home.</p>
<p><strong>A good record keeps the instructions and what actually happened.</strong> It doesn't turn the number into an app score. Keep these with the original lab report:</p>
<div class="not-prose my-6 grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-2">
  <div class={card}>
    <p class={cardTitle}>Collection</p>
    <p class={cardText}>Sample type, the time you were asked to collect and the time you actually did.</p>
    <p class={cardMuted}><strong>Ask:</strong> was this collected the way the plan intended?</p>
  </div>
  <div class={card}>
    <p class={cardTitle}>Preparation</p>
    <p class={cardText}>Any step you missed, and the medicines you've already told the doctor about.</p>
    <p class={cardMuted}><strong>Ask:</strong> could this change the result, or mean I need another sample?</p>
  </div>
  <div class={card}>
    <p class={cardTitle}>Report</p>
    <p class={cardText}>Lab name, result, units and reference range, exactly as printed.</p>
    <p class={cardMuted}><strong>Ask:</strong> which range applies to this sample and collection time?</p>
  </div>
  <div class={card}>
    <p class={cardTitle}>Next step</p>
    <p class={cardText}>Who will explain the result, and when.</p>
    <p class={cardMuted}><strong>Ask:</strong> do I need a follow-up, and for what medical question?</p>
  </div>
</div>
<p>This is a template to keep things organized. It is not a tested diagnostic tool.</p>
<p>Follow your kit's instructions, and don't change medicines without advice from the person who prescribed them. Keep reports that identify you private. You don't need to post them online to use this checklist.</p>

<h2>Questions to ask before buying a home cortisol kit</h2>
<ul>
  <li>What medical question is this test meant to answer?</li>
  <li>Does it need a doctor's order, and who reviews abnormal results?</li>
  <li>Which lab tests it, and with which method?</li>
  <li>Are the timing, storage and shipping instructions clear?</li>
  <li>Does the price include someone explaining the result and follow-up, or just a report?</li>
  <li>How are my sample details and results stored and shared?</li>
</ul>
<p>What's available, the price and whether you need an order all vary by provider and location.</p>
<p>We don't call any brand "most accurate" without evidence comparing them. We also don't recommend a routine testing package for everyday stress.</p>

