<h2>How should I interpret a home cortisol result?</h2>
<p><strong>Keep the test type, collection time, units and lab range together.</strong></p>
<p>Cortisol changes during the day, and medicines and health conditions can affect the result. A result outside the reference range is a reason to talk it through with the right doctor. It is not a reason to diagnose yourself or start taking supplements.</p>
<p><strong>Don't stop prescribed steroid medicine to change a test result.</strong> Talk to the prescriber or the doctor who ordered the test about how to prepare. NIDDK explains why changes to glucocorticoid (steroid) treatment need medical supervision.</p>

<ZenPicture src="/app-ui/start/zen_explain.webp" caption="A lab result and an app score are two different things." />

<h2>Can I track cortisol at home without a kit?</h2>
<p><strong>You can keep a log, but it won't show your hormone level.</strong> Try a <a href="/cortisol/track/">stress, sleep and wearable observation log</a>.</p>
<p>Cortisol+ turns the wearable data it can get into a wellness score. It can't check a home test, replace follow-up care, or tell you whether your cortisol is high or low. Read <a href="/how-it-works/">how the score works</a>.</p>

<h2>Do I need to keep retesting?</h2>
<p><strong>Ask the doctor who is reading your results.</strong> There is no single retesting schedule that suits everyone.</p>
<p>If you want to understand your daily routine, the <a href="/resources/stress-sleep-journal/">free observation journal</a> can help you organize your questions without buying test after test.</p>

<h2>What comes after testing?</h2>
<p><strong>The next step depends on the results and your medical situation.</strong></p>
<p>For general stress-management habits, read <a href="/cortisol/lower/">how to lower cortisol levels naturally: evidence and limits</a>. Lifestyle tips don't replace treatment for a diagnosed hormone (endocrine) disorder.</p>
</ArticleLayout>
